<% content_for :title, 'Star Survey' %>

<%= render 'help/page', title: 'Star Survey' do %>
  <div class='space-y-6'>
      <div>
        <p class='text-sm font-semibold text-highlight mb-2'>Star Map</p>
        <dl class='space-y-5 text-sm'>
          <div>
            <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Schematic Map</dt>
            <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>
              The same style of map as on the star system page, but showing only what orbits this star. A star
              orbiting this one appears as a single node, without the objects that orbit it &mdash; open that star&rsquo;s
              own page to see them. Click any object to open its page. Not drawn to scale.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Copy Public Map Link</dt>
            <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>
              Link icon above the map &mdash; copies a signed, no-login-required link to this star&rsquo;s schematic map
              as an SVG image. For a WebP image instead, put
              <span class='font-mono text-fg-bright'>.webp</span> straight after
              <span class='font-mono text-fg-bright'>/map</span> in the copied address.
            </dd>
          </div>
        </dl>
      </div>

      <div>
        <p class='text-sm font-semibold text-highlight mb-2'>Orbit Map</p>
        <dl class='space-y-5 text-sm'>
          <div>
            <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Opening</dt>
            <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>
              Solar system icon beside the copy link icon. Opens the orbit map in a window, in the current theme.
              Click any object to open its page; the star at the centre is not a link.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Layout</dt>
            <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>
              The star sits at the centre with the objects orbiting it on rings, nearest first. The same map as the
              interactive starmap. Rings are evenly spaced in order of distance rather than drawn to scale, and the
              real gaps between orbits nudge the spacing. Each object keeps its real bearing from the star. The
              dashed outline of each ring is that object&rsquo;s orbit, so an eccentric orbit shows as a stretched
              ellipse.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Labels</dt>
            <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>
              Each object shows its name, or its orbit sequence letter if it has none, with its real distance from the
              star in AU underneath.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Distant Companions</dt>
            <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>
              A companion or secondary star orbiting far beyond the planetary system is pinned to the outermost ring,
              clear of the other objects.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Belts</dt>
            <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>
              Planetoids that belong to a belt shown on the map are drawn as small rocks scattered around that
              belt&rsquo;s ring, rather than taking rings of their own.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Jump Shadow</dt>
            <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>
              The glowing ring is the boundary of the star&rsquo;s jump shadow, placed between the two rings it falls
              between. A companion or secondary star shown on the map gets its own ring around it, in its own colour.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Crowded Stars</dt>
            <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>
              The map grows with the number of rings so that they stay legible. A large map scrolls inside the window.
              Labels can still overlap where two neighbouring objects lie in nearly the same direction.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Survey Index</dt>
            <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>
              Unlike the interactive starmap, the orbit map shows every object regardless of the system&rsquo;s survey
              index.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>Download</dt>
            <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>
              Downloads the orbit map as a WebP image, without links, in the current theme. It is named after the
              star, or after the star system if the star has no name, or after the sector and hex if neither has one.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt class='font-semibold text-fg-bright'>API</dt>
            <dd class='mt-1 text-fg'>
              The orbit map is also available as an SVG or WebP image from the API, for use in other tools &mdash; see
              API Access in Campaign Settings.
            </dd>
          </div>
        </dl>
      </div>

      <div class='pt-2 text-right uppercase font-mono text-xs tracking-[0.22em] text-fg-muted'>
        REFERENCE 1009ST
      </div>
  </div>
<% end %>
